From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 18:33:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Keep the lowres intra estimates of blocks the caller
 marks constant

The pre-lookahead estimates an intra cost and mode for every 8x8 block of a frame's lowres
picture, 30 predictions and SATDs a block over the whole picture, on one thread, before the
frame reaches the encoder. A block the picture's constant map marks, whose neighbours the
estimate reads samples from (left, above-left, above, above-right, below-left) are marked as
well, has the lowres samples the frame before had, so the estimate would come out the same: it
keeps the frame before's estimate and mode where the lookahead's last non-B frame, alive as the
reference, is the frame before. With a lookahead it stands further back, so a block it holds
the estimate of can have changed since, and the estimate is made anew. The estimate is kept in
a Lowres array of its own (intraEstimate): a VBV rate control weights intraCost, and an I
frame's intra lowres costs, in place by each block's quantizer offset as the frame is coded, so
a block taken from there would carry the weight of adaptive quantization or of a caller's
offset into the next frame's estimate and compound it every frame it stays constant.

With no lookahead and no B-frames, where the lookahead's last non-B frame is the frame before,
every stream comes out bit for bit as without the patch, at 1920x1080 on one thread with a
still screen marked beside a moving window: at a constant quantizer, a constant rate factor,
and a constant rate, with adaptive quantization at strength 1 and, through qp-offsets, with
offsets on constant blocks, on blocks refined before they turn constant, on key frames, and
with none. A constant rate on several threads differs from run to run in x265 itself; there
every frame's estimates come out the same. With a lookahead, every estimate is made anew and
the stream is the unpatched one's. A still 1920x1080 frame, every block marked, took 3.0 ms on
eight threads where it took 4.5, and 7.2 on one where it took 9.6; at 2560x1600 6.1 against 8.8
on eight.
